deps-restorer, install: name a replaced tree that can't be deleted, and sweep it later (homescoop#1532)

0010/0011 rename the old tree aside, rename the staged one in, and then
delete the old one. When that delete fails (an entry pnpm can't unlink, such
as a file in a read-only directory), the old tree stayed as a
<name>_pacquet-stage_* sibling with only a tracing event no reporter shows,
and no later install ever looked at it. Now the failure is named on stderr
and the leftover's path is recorded in .pacquet-stage-leftovers in the
outermost modules directory above it (the outermost one holding a
.modules.yaml, else the outermost node_modules, else its parent). Every
install that touches node_modules (not --lockfile-only or --dry-run) sweeps
the records of the root's modules directory and of each project's (its
modulesDir, and the node_modules the hoisted linker nests into): the full
install before its repeat-install check, and the CLI's synchronous "Already
up to date" fast path before it reports. Only recorded entries are deleted:
a recorded line must be relative, plain (no `..`, no root), named like
pick_stage_path's <name>_pacquet-stage_<pid>_<nanos>_<n>, and reached
through real directories, not symlinks. An entry pnpm didn't record is
never deleted, whatever its name (a real package may be called that). One
that still can't be deleted is named again and stays recorded.

Upstream: not filed (Lars decides).

